@startuml NIST FedRAMP Combined Resolution
title 2. Two dimensions: NIST baseline and FedRAMP baseline combined for one component

artifact "NIST/NIST_SP-800-53_rev5_catalog.yaml\n(catalog)" as catalog
artifact "NIST/NIST_..._HIGH-baseline_profile.yaml\n(profile — NIST's own\nHIGH baseline)" as nist_high
artifact "FedRAMP/FedRAMP_..._HIGH-baseline_profile.yaml\n(profile — FedRAMP's HIGH\nbaseline, tailored independently)" as fedramp_high
artifact "combining-profile.yaml\n(profile, imports both)" as combiner
artifact "combined-resolved-catalog.yaml\n(resolved catalog)" as combined
artifact "example-component-mongodb-fedramp-high.yaml\nMongoDB (service)" as component

catalog --> nist_high : import + tailor
catalog --> fedramp_high : import + tailor

nist_high --> combiner : import #1
fedramp_high --> combiner : import #2

combiner --> combined : merge + modify\n(reconcile conflicts)
combined --> component : implements

note right of combiner
  Both profiles tailor the same NIST
  catalog, so overlapping control IDs
  (e.g. ac-2.3) can collide. merge.combine
  method plus explicit modify.set-parameters
  decide which value wins.
end note

note right of component
  Two control-implementations, one
  per source profile, both against
  control-id ac-2.3 — the same
  collision the note above calls
  out. NIST HIGH leaves the disable/
  inactivity timers organization-
  defined; FedRAMP HIGH fixes them
  to 24h/35 days and adds a JAB/AO-
  approved requirement for non-user
  and device-identifier accounts.
  No merged catalog file exists;
  the component cites both source
  profiles directly instead.
end note

@enduml
